# Git push policy

**The user pushes to GitHub themselves. Never push code.**

## Never (without an explicit, unambiguous user request in the current turn)

- `git push` (any remote, branch, or tag) — including `--force`, `--tags`, `-u`
- `gh pr create` / `gh pr merge` / `gh release create` — anything that writes to the remote
- Any command that publishes a branch (`git push -u origin HEAD`, `gh repo sync`, …)

Pushing `main` triggers CI + production deploy (`11-deploy-ops.mdc`) — treat it as a deploy action, not a save.

## Allowed

| Action | When |
|--------|------|
| Edit files in the working tree | Normal work |
| `git status` / `git diff` / `git log` | Always (read-only) |
| `git add` + `git commit` | **Only** when the user asks to commit |
| Report what is staged / unstaged | End of a task |

## Default end-of-task behavior

Leave changes **uncommitted** in the working tree, run the ship checks
(`npm run lint && npm run check:urdu && npm run test` — `preflight` skill), then summarize:

```
Changes are uncommitted on <branch>. Push when you're ready.
```

Do not offer to push. If a commit-and-push action is requested (e.g. a diff-tab
action), commit only and tell the user the push is theirs to run.

## Fix

```bash
# ❌ BAD — agent publishes to remote
git commit -m "…" && git push origin HEAD

# ✅ GOOD — agent stops at the commit the user asked for
git commit -m "…"
git status -sb   # report: ahead of origin/main by 1 commit
```

